            <!-- You can paste/update the PDF text here; this is the structure we used earlier -->
            <h3>Programming</h3>
            <p>The process of giving a computer a set of specific, step-by-step instructions to perform a task or solve a problem. <em>Coding</em> is another word for programming.</p>

            <h3>Code</h3>
            <p>The actual instructions given to a computer.</p>
            <ul><li>Code is written in a specialized programming language that the computer can understand.</li></ul>

            <h3>Some real-life objects that use programming</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Parts of cars</li><li>Smartphones</li><li>Calculators</li><li>And many more!</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Programming Process</h3>
            <p>The process used to program a computer to do a specific task.</p>
            <ul>
              <li>Have a clear idea of what you want the program to do.</li>
              <li><strong>Step 1:</strong> Identify the problem or task — what does the robot need to accomplish?</li>
              <li><strong>Step 2:</strong> Describe a series of actions that solves the problem (can be done using pseudocode, taught later).</li>
              <li><strong>Step 3:</strong> Write your program — choose a programming language and describe what you want the computer to do.</li>
              <li><strong>Step 4:</strong> Test and improve — debugging means finding and fixing errors (“bugs”).</li>
              <li>Sometimes you’ll need to return to Step 2 to refine your actions.</li>
            </ul>
